window.COACH = {"page": "human-practices", "title": "Integrated Human Practices", "prizeName": "Best Integrated Human Practices", "summary": {"verdict": "The raw material is at winner level: about 18 dated expert meetings, Before/After lists, second visits that report what changed, and a bioreactor story told as a chain of questions. The writing around it holds the page back: a generic opening, entries printed up to three times, change slots full of 'we learned' and 'consider', claims in the survey and Conclusion sections that go past the evidence, and several facts that disagree with the Entrepreneurship, Laws and Data Physicalization pages.", "strengths": ["Bridge paragraphs between entries say what you still needed and why the next person, so meetings read as a chain. The Bioreactor and hardware pipeline is the best example.", "Continuous engagement and the best Before/After lists name concrete changes: osmotic stress dropped, soil release abandoned, plate-to-soil model adopted, protectant tested on plants before going into the bacteria.", "Honest reporting of problems: unintended stress in early plant results, low protein yield, membrane leakage on 4 September.", "Outputs traced to people: the LINE platform to the Farmer Expo and Ms. Chen, the forum format to Green Media, the threat analysis to Mr. Chen.", "A wide range of voices: smallholder and natural farmers, academics in Taiwan and at Stanford, two companies, an NGO, and the public."], "priorities": ["Fix the facts that disagree with other pages: kill switch, target stresses, expansion countries, the printer-and-ink analogy, the target farmer, and which booth the 62-person survey covered.", "Rewrite the abstract sheet and the opening around your own problem and your biggest changes. The lead sentence defines Human Practices, the bold \"seven months, March to September\" is wrong, and two Engagements lines miscount (five experts, three events). Cut the Human Practices definition in the body too.", "Print each stakeholder entry once, and rewrite every change slot as an action (changed, dropped, added, chose) with dates and numbers.", "Add a short context and prior-work paragraph and a limitations section, and answer the open questions on the page (the farmers' two questions, which stresses, kill switch or not).", "Cut claims the data cannot carry: 'every stakeholder', 'fully tested', 'without ecological risks', 'confirm' for self-ratings, and 'increased' without a before-survey."], "ballot": [{"question": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?", "status": "strong", "where": "2. Project evolution map; 3.1 Pipelines; 3.2 Continuous engagement", "note": "Dated meetings from February to September with Before/After lists and second visits show input all season; mismatched stage dates and vague change slots hide some of it."}, {"question": "How inspiring an example is it to others?", "status": "partial", "where": "4.2 LINE platform; 5. Public engagement", "note": "The farmer LINE tool and the forum format are original, but the opening gives no personal stake and the page reports no usage numbers for the tool."}, {"question": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?", "status": "partial", "where": "3.1 Pipelines; 4.2 LINE platform; 5.2 Booth activities", "note": "Entries follow one template and the booths are described well enough to copy, but there are no downloadable question lists, survey forms or platform files."}, {"question": "How thoughtfully was it implemented? How well did they explain the context, rationale and prior work?", "status": "partial", "where": "Bridge paragraphs in 3.1 Pipelines", "note": "The reason for each meeting is clear, but no section gives the context or prior work the Conclusion claims, and there is no limitations section."}, {"question": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Expert engagement; 4. Farmer engagement; 5.1 Public forum", "note": "The range of voices is wide, but conflicting advice (pests versus heat, drought versus salinity, how much to intervene) is never weighed and settled on the page, and no regulator was interviewed."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1. Overview", "2. Project evolution map", "3. Expert engagement (3.1 Pipelines in six tabs, 3.2 Continuous engagement)", "4. Farmer engagement (4.1 Farmer interviews, 4.2 LINE platform)", "5. Public engagement (5.1 Public forum, 5.2 Booth activities, 5.3 Plant stress event)", "6. Conclusion"], "winners": ["Opening that states the team's own problem and HP stance", "Stakeholder index: timeline cards, map or summary cards", "Argued narrative where stakeholders cause design decisions, ordered by phase, design question or pivot", "Outputs beyond the lab: guides, tools, education, community resources", "Limitations or negative impacts, next steps, conclusion"], "gap": "ReLeaf has every part except limitations: the evolution map is the index, the pipeline bridges are the argued narrative, and LINE and the forum are outputs. The argued story is split across six tabs with entries repeated, so the decision chain is harder to follow than in NYU Abu Dhabi or WageningenUR, and the page ends on claims with no section on limits or harms."}, "extras": ["\"sets off the green life\" (three times): should be \"sets off the green light\".", "\"commercialization protectants\" (in every CH Biotech copy): add \"of\", so it reads \"commercialization of protectants\".", "\"B. Subtilis powder or liquid\" (three times): the species name takes a small s, \"B. subtilis\".", "\"Design a kill switch to prevent B. sub leakage and cause pollution\" (twice): as written, the kill switch causes pollution. Write \"Design a kill switch to stop B. subtilis leaking out and causing pollution\".", "\"World. Veg\": should be \"the World Vegetable Center\".", "\"can’t assess the resources\": \"assess\" means judge. You mean \"access\" (reach), so write \"cannot easily access the resources\".", "\"B. subtilis. shifted toward designing\": this sentence has no subject. Write \"For the dry lab, we moved toward designing shorter or modified peptides...\".", "\"“An on-site, on-demand bioreactor for smarter crop protection.\": the slogan has no closing quotation mark. Add ” after \"protection.\".", "\"After the second meeting professor Chen\": write \"After the second meeting with Professor Chen\", and end that paragraph with a full stop after \"CH Biotech\".", "\"including the speaker. farmers, and the general public\": the full stop should be a comma, \"the speaker, farmers and the general public\".", "\"supporting local Taiwanese cocoa farming\" under the heading \"Coffee infographic\": say coffee, cocoa or both, and make the heading match.", "\"six interactive stations, covering coffee farming, plant stress maps, drought sound recordings...\": section 5.2 lists coffee, project introduction, data physicalization, ring toss, online game and crafts. Use the same six names in both places.", "\"March 27, 2026\" (and every date in 3.2 Continuous engagement): the rest of the page writes \"27 March\". Use one date style on the whole wiki.", "\"Green Media (綠媒體), 5 September\" on the map, but \"meeting with Green Media in early July\" in 5.1: give the date of the July meeting, or label the map card as the forum day.", "\"8/16 light cycle\" (twice): say which number is hours of light, for example \"16 h light, 8 h dark\" or \"8 h light, 16 h dark\".", "\"B. subtilis might be intentionally released\": check what Mr. Chen meant. If he meant by accident, write \"accidentally released\"; if he meant on purpose, say who might do it.", "\"DNA (>10k)\" (three times): add the unit, for example \"more than 10,000 ng\" or \"longer than 10 kb\", so another team can repeat it.", "\"LEA14, NHP, and BoPep4\": NHP appears only here and is never explained. Spell it out or remove it. The BIO Asia entry lists \"ACCD, LEA14, and BoPep4\", so make the two lists match."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["human-practices", "education", "entrepreneurship", "sustainability", "laws-and-regulations", "geospatial-analysis", "data-physicalization"], "wiki": "c79a7ce, 2026-10-13", "notes": [{"id": "hp-a06", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "continuous feedback loops with five core experts", "title": "Five promised, four shown", "body": "Section 3.2 shows four experts: Prof. Cheng, Prof. Chen, Prof. Chang and Dr. Sattely. Either add Prof. Huang Chieh-Chen, who also met you twice, or write \"four\".", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-a08", "type": "clarify", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "to make our resources more accessible to users", "title": "Say what the platform is", "body": "Read alone, this line does not say what the platform does or who it is for. Most judges outside Taiwan also do not know LINE. Name the users and one or two features.", "after": "LINE platform · a help account for farmers on LINE, a chat app widely used in Taiwan, with stress alerts, crop advice and seed swaps", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-002", "type": "cut", "anchor": "In our project ReLEAF, Human Practices acts as the heart of the team", "title": "Cut the definition of Human Practices", "body": "This paragraph explains what Human Practices is for. The judges wrote that definition, so it tells them nothing new. Keep one sentence at most.", "after": "Human Practices let us check each lab choice against what farmers need.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-006", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Gilbert Nkpeniyeng, 1 February", "title": "Cards sit in the wrong stage", "body": "Stage 01 says March to April, but its first card is 1 February. Stage 04 says September, but it holds cards from 16 May, 18 July and 21 July, and Mr. Chen has no date at all. Either widen the stage dates or move each card to the stage its date belongs to.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "hp-011", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Through this meeting, we understood the challenges the smallholder farmers are facing", "title": "Make the change slot about action", "body": "Across the page, 'Impact and adaptation' is often filled with 'we understood', 'we learned' or 'we realized'. Those verbs leave the loop open. Rename the slot 'What we changed' and start it with a verb like changed, dropped, added or chose.", "after": "What we changed: we chose a social enterprise model because Gilbert showed us [one fact about the Ghana market]. This means [who pays and how].", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-h01", "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both end each entry on a decision, under a label that asks for one.", "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "hp-012", "type": "praise", "anchor": "But after having conversations with actual organic farmers at a farmers’ market, we learned that it might not be the case", "title": "You report a wrong guess honestly", "body": "You say what you assumed (organic farmers are the target), what showed it might be wrong, and who you asked next. Admitting a wrong guess is strong proof that people changed your project. Queen's does the same on its 2025 Education page.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-ug-queens-04"]}, {"id": "hp-013", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "as these factors may increase our product’s acceptance among traditional and natural farmers", "title": "Who is the target farmer now?", "body": "The page doubts organic farmers as the target, then never says who replaced them. The Entrepreneurship page still sizes its obtainable market as about 4.7 million organic farms. Decide, then say it the same way on both pages.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-014", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "which influenced our decision to expand into Southeast Asia", "title": "Expansion countries differ between pages", "body": "The BIO Asia entry lists the Philippines, Malaysia and the EU. The Entrepreneurship page says the same visit led you to the Philippines and Indonesia. The Laws page orders Taiwan, EU, Philippines, Vietnam, Thailand, Indonesia. Pick one list and use it on all three pages.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-016", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Talking with them also allowed us to receive confirmation that government sectors are willing to help", "title": "End the Agriweek entry with a change", "body": "This entry has no change slot, and 'government sectors' is vague: who told you, and from which agency? The Entrepreneurship page says this visit set your pricing. Say that here too.", "after": "A [role] from the [agency] told us that [what they said about subsidies]. What we changed: we set our price at [price], using BioBar's and AgriWeather's prices as a guide.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-017", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "9 conversations · 19 March to 28 August", "title": "Print each entry once", "body": "The same entries appear in full in several tabs: CH Biotech and BIO Asia three times each, Prof. Chen's June meeting three times, and Brophy, both Huang meetings and Yes Health twice. A judge who opens every tab reads them again, and slips get copied too ('sets off the green life' for green light appears three times). Keep the full entry in one tab. In the others, keep your bridge paragraph and a link to it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-018", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "To simulate stress, we should only add water every few days for salinity", "title": "Which method is for which stress?", "body": "Watering less mimics drought, so it does not match 'salinity', and the larger 'particles' are not named. As written, nobody could repeat it. Give one method per stress and name what you add.", "after": "To mimic salt stress, add [salt and concentration]. To mimic drought, water only every [number] days, or add [substance] that holds water back from the roots.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-020", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Dr. Verslues shifted our direction from simply delivering more protective compounds to regulating the plant’s natural response.", "title": "One pivot, described two ways", "body": "His bio says he moved you 'from stress protection to stress detection'. This bridge says from delivering compounds to regulating the plant's own response. Those are two different changes. Say which one happened, and use the same words in his bio.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-022", "type": "praise", "anchor": "However, as we incorporated feedback from different experts, we realized that some of our experimental results might have been influenced by unintentional stress.", "title": "You admit early results may be flawed", "body": "Saying that some early results may reflect handling stress is honest, and it explains why you went back to Prof. Cheng. NYU Abu Dhabi earns trust the same way, by separating what was shown from what was not.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-05"]}, {"id": "hp-023", "type": "praise", "anchor": "However, we grew concerned that supplying external proline might suppress the plant's natural proline biosynthesis genes", "title": "You state your worry before the meeting", "body": "You name the plan, the risk you suspected, and why that sent you to Dr. Verslues. The reader knows the question before the answer. NYU Abu Dhabi's pivot paragraphs start the same way.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-02"]}, {"id": "hp-024", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "testing precursor molecules and focusing our validation on root growth and biomass", "title": "Which precursors did you test?", "body": "This says you pivoted to testing precursor molecules. No precursor is named anywhere else on this page, and the Laws page lists only ACCD, AtLEA14 and BoPep4. Name them and link the result, or say the idea was dropped.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-025", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "also advised us to explore other types of molecules such as peptides for our project", "title": "Check the order of the peptide idea", "body": "BoPep4 is a peptide, and you list it as a candidate before this 27 June meeting. So did Dr. Sattely suggest peptides, or confirm a choice you had already made? Say which, with the date BoPep4 joined your list.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-026", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Use the results of direct plant assays to determine whether a protectant is worth integrating", "title": "A clear rule for the next step", "body": "This After point is a decision rule: test the protectant on plants first, and only then build it into the bacteria. Your photos from 4 and 22 August show you followed it. That is what 'integrated' means to a judge.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "hp-027", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Building on this insight, we re-evaluated how we test our protectant candidates under field-relevant conditions.", "title": "Move these changes into the After list", "body": "The BIO Asia entry's After list holds only hay infusion, but this paragraph and the entry's Impact slot name bigger changes: soil-based trials, an oxygen sensor, a larger sample size. Put them in After, with dates.", "after": "After:\n- Added dissolved oxygen and pH sensors to the reactor (on the bench 5 August)\n- Moved [which] protectant tests from agar to soil\n- Raised sample size from [n] to [n]\n- Tested hay infusion as a cheap feedstock (3 August)", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-028", "type": "praise", "anchor": "While working on the initial ideas of our bioreactor, we ran into a challenge.", "title": "The problem comes before the person", "body": "You open the pipeline with the problem (bacteria could leak into soil and food), then bring in Dr. Brophy as the answer. A reader follows a problem much more easily than a list of names. JU-Krakow handles its public-panic risk this way.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-04"]}, {"id": "hp-029", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "running our genetic circuit continuously could lead to excessively high protectant concentrations that harm host cells", "title": "Who is harmed: plant or bacteria?", "body": "His 28 July entry says too much protectant 'may affect plant health'. This paragraph says it harms 'host cells', which means your B. subtilis. Check your notes and say which he meant, or both.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-031", "type": "praise", "anchor": "However, this raised a new question: how could we prevent the engineered B. subtilis from escaping", "title": "Each answer raises the next question", "body": "The Bioreactor pipeline is the best-told part of the page. Each bridge ends on a new question, and the next expert answers it: Chen, then Brophy, then Chang, then back to Chen. WageningenUR builds its whole story this way.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-02", "ihp-2025-og-wur-04"], "excerpt_note": "Look for 'This led us to' and 'Instead': the same hand-offs you use.", "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "hp-032", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "who challenged our original reliance on a kill switch and recommended making physical containment the primary safety mechanism", "title": "Kill switch: yes or no?", "body": "This page says you may drop the kill switch (Dr. Lin) and are still 'evaluating whether an additional kill switch is necessary' (Dr. Brophy). The Entrepreneurship page says the reactor has 'redundant genetic kill switches', including a CRISPR one. Decide what is true today and say it the same way on both pages.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-033", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "pointed out the potential issue of membrane caking", "title": "Explain caking and transmembrane pressure", "body": "Most judges will not know these two terms. A few plain words in brackets keep them reading.", "after": "...pointed out that cells can build up on the membrane and block it (caking). Rising transmembrane pressure, the push needed to move liquid through the membrane, is the early warning.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-034", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Prof. Chang provided us with valuable advice on how to try our best to solve these issues", "title": "Replace 'valuable advice' with the advice", "body": "This sentence says advice was given but not what it was. Your Continuous engagement section has it: pore size and membrane integrity, AIoT, and a replaceable capsule. Use that here.", "after": "Prof. Chang walked us through pore size and membrane integrity to find the cause of the leak.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-036", "type": "cut", "anchor": "that human practices is an ongoing dialogue that never ends, not just one time meetings", "title": "Start with what you did", "body": "The first sentence states a general lesson. The second sentence already shows it with facts, so start there.", "after": "Our project only took real shape when we went back to experts with prototypes, new wet-lab data and updated circuit designs.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-037", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "By setting up continuous feedback loops with five core experts", "title": "Five experts promised, four shown", "body": "This section shows four people: Prof. Cheng, Prof. Chen, Prof. Chang and Dr. Sattely. Prof. Huang Chieh-Chen also met you twice but is not here. Either add him or write 'four'.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-039", "type": "praise", "anchor": "dropped osmotic stress to avoid confounding variables, adopted a plate-to-soil transition model", "title": "Four changes, four action verbs", "body": "Shifted, dropped, adopted, incorporated: every verb here is a change a judge can check against your Plants page. Dropping osmotic stress is the strongest kind of proof, an option removed because of advice. Use this as the standard for every change slot.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-01", "ihp-2023-ug-hust-02"], "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "hp-040", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "This continuous interaction helped us further refine our plant cultivation protocols", "title": "Make the second visit's change concrete", "body": "Her first visit has four clear changes, but this one stays general. Your 28 August entry already has specifics: fixed plant age, treatment timing, fewer transfers. Bring them here, with numbers.", "after": "We fixed plant age at [days] for all assays, set treatment timing to [time], and cut transfers between agar, soil and water to [number].", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-01"], "excerpt_note": "Every change is written as before and after, with units."}, {"id": "hp-042", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "we narrowed our focus to heat and salinity stress, prioritized direct plant assays", "title": "Target stresses differ across the wiki", "body": "Here you narrowed to heat and salinity, and earlier you dropped osmotic stress after Prof. Cheng. The Laws page still claims AtLEA14 protects against drought and osmotic stress. Make the stress list match on every page.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-046", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "This event significantly shaped our project direction by not only focusing on abiotic but also on biotic stresses", "title": "Show how you settled the stress conflict", "body": "Farmers here asked you to add pests and disease. World Vegetable Center told you to stay with heat, and Dr. Sattely told you to pick one stress. The page never says which view won. Put the views side by side and say what you chose and why.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-h03", "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-03"], "excerpt_note": "Both weigh one stakeholder against another by name and say what that meant for the plan.", "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "hp-048", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Through the discussion, we learned that farmers might have concerns about the effectiveness of the technology solutions", "title": "Split this long sentence", "body": "This sentence runs about 55 words with four ideas in it. Short sentences make her point land, and it is one of the best points on the page.", "after": "Ms. Chen worried that too much help makes plants weaker. A plant that is always protected may never build its own resilience. So we [set a dose limit of X / release protectant only when stress passes a threshold].", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-h04"], "excerpt_note": "A farmer's complaint becomes a property the team can tune."}, {"id": "hp-049", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Therefore, we designed a seed exchange platform that will allow us to track the owners of the seeds.", "title": "Two descriptions of the seed platform", "body": "Here the platform lets you 'track the owners of the seeds'. In section 4.2 it lets farmers post and match seed offers, and it was Ms. Chen's idea. Tracking owners and matching swaps are different tools. Use the 4.2 description here.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-050", "type": "praise", "anchor": "In response, we incorporated this potential risk into the threat analysis of our business plan", "title": "A risk, a response, a place", "body": "Mr. Chen raised real-world risks (storms, animals, release), and you say exactly where they went: the threat analysis in your business plan. A link to that section would close the loop fully.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-og-uzh-02"], "excerpt_note": "A critic's concerns become concrete obligations.", "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "hp-052", "type": "praise", "anchor": "However, as our website is not intended to replace professional agricultural expertise", "title": "You set a limit on your own tool", "body": "Telling farmers to check official sources before acting is a responsible limit, and judges notice it. SDU-Denmark does the same when it says what its product must not claim.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2023-og-sdu-03"]}, {"id": "hp-053", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "we incorporated a Google Form to collect suggestions and areas for improvement", "title": "Report who has used the platform", "body": "The section describes features but no users. How many people follow the account, and how many posts or form replies came in? Even small numbers show farmers used it.", "after": "By [date], [number] people had added 農友助手 and [number] answered the feedback form. The most requested change was [x].", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03"], "ballot": "How inspiring an example is it to others?"}, {"id": "hp-054", "type": "praise", "anchor": "we structured the forum to ensure that every participant’s voice could be heard", "title": "The forum format came from advice", "body": "You say what Green Media told you in July and how it shaped the forum. That makes the event itself a result of stakeholder input.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-056", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Their insights helped us to further improve our design.", "title": "Say how each expert changed the design", "body": "This line promises design changes, but the three paragraphs below only report what each expert said. Add one 'what we changed' line to each, or remove the promise.", "after": "Dr. Li: [what changed]. Prof. Chen: [what changed]. CH Biotech: [what changed].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-057", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "the goal should be to find a balance between growth and defense", "title": "Your strongest theme deserves one paragraph", "body": "Ms. Chen, Dr. Li, Prof. Chen and CH Biotech all warned against too much intervention. Four voices, from farmer to industry, saying one thing is your clearest social finding. Write one paragraph that links it to your design: dose limits, periodic release, seedling timing.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-og-uzh-02"], "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "hp-058", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The first concerned whether organic certification is required when using our bioreactor.", "title": "Answer the farmers' two questions", "body": "Farmers asked about organic certification and how many reactors a farm needs. The page records the questions but never the answers. Give your answer, even if it is 'we do not know yet, and here is how we will find out'.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-02"], "excerpt_note": "An audience question leads to a clear decision on the page.", "ballot": "How thoughtfully was it implemented? How well did they explain the context, rationale and prior work?"}, {"id": "hp-059", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "and willingness to support local smallholder farmers and their agricultural products were all increased.", "title": "No before-survey, so no 'increase'", "body": "You only asked after the forum, so the data cannot show an increase. Also give how many people answered in each group (Fig. 1 seems to be one speaker). Report what you measured.", "after": "After the forum, [n] members of the public answered. [x]% agreed that [statement]. We did not survey before the forum, so we cannot measure a change.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg asks before and after, which is what lets it claim a change."}, {"id": "hp-060", "type": "praise", "anchor": "For our ring toss booth, we wanted to help people understand the difference between biotic and abiotic stress", "title": "A booth another team can copy", "body": "You give the goal, the materials (labelled bottles, coloured rings) and the rule (match ring to stressor) in three sentences. A teacher could run this tomorrow.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-ug-queens-06"], "ballot": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?"}, {"id": "hp-061", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Through this survey, we hope to capture a definitive reflection of our project’s ultimate impact", "title": "Cut the survey promise", "body": "This paragraph is mostly hopes ('definitive reflection', 'ultimate impact', 'more light to be shone'). The method fits in one sentence.", "after": "After the booths, we gave visitors a KAP survey (62 responses).", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-ug-queens-07"], "excerpt_note": "Even winners slip here: Queen's calls 6,000 views 'genuine dialogue'."}, {"id": "hp-062", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Our survey results from 62 respondents demonstrate that our interactive booth effectively communicated", "title": "Which booth was surveyed?", "body": "The Data Physicalization page says 62 of 116 visitors to its booth answered this survey. Here the same 62 stand for all six booths. Say which it was, and give the 116 if it applies.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-063", "type": "cut", "anchor": "these metrics confirm that our hands-on games, interactive simulations, and station presentations successfully broke down", "title": "Self-ratings do not confirm learning", "body": "Visitors rated their own understanding, and nobody was tested. Write what the numbers are and drop 'confirm'. The same goes for the trust and 'active advocates' claims further down.", "after": "Fewer than 5% of visitors rated their understanding as low.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-065", "type": "cut", "anchor": "every stakeholder’s insights directly impacted and led to changes in research direction", "title": "Claim only what your entries show", "body": "Several entries end with no change: Taiwan SMART Agriweek, the NCHU Symposium and Prof. Huang Tzu-Pi. 'Every' is easy to disprove.", "after": "Across [number] conversations, stakeholder input changed our research direction, our plant model and our hardware.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-06"], "excerpt_note": "Even winners slip here: Bielefeld's conclusion claims more than its own entries."}, {"id": "hp-066", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "We provided clear context for each stage of ReLEAF by examining existing agricultural methods, prior work", "title": "Show the context you say you gave", "body": "The ballot asks how well you explain context, rationale and prior work. This sentence claims it, but no section shows it: what protectants farmers use now, earlier iGEM teams with soil bacteria, Taiwan's GMO rules. Add a short context paragraph after the Overview, with sources.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-03", "ihp-2023-ug-hust-03"], "excerpt_note": "Both explain the background a judge needs in plain words, with a source.", "ballot": "How thoughtfully was it implemented? How well did they explain the context, rationale and prior work?"}, {"id": "hp-067", "type": "cut", "anchor": "ensuring our solution actively protects crops under climate stress without introducing ecological risks", "title": "Limit the safety claim to what you tested", "body": "Membrane leakage was still open on 4 September, and the kill switch question is not settled. 'Without introducing ecological risks' cannot be shown yet. SDU-Denmark limits its claim to exactly what was tested.", "after": "...we chose physical containment as our main safety layer. We are still testing the membrane for leaks.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2023-og-sdu-03"]}, {"id": "hp-068", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "In previous pages, we have also mapped out our entire Human Practices journey on this page", "title": "This sentence contradicts itself", "body": "'In previous pages' and 'on this page' cannot both be true, and the next sentence repeats the same idea. Keep one short sentence.", "after": "This page maps every interaction and the step we took after it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "hp-069", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "By transparently detailing our successes, challenges, and methodology, we provide an accessible, step-by-step roadmap", "title": "Give others something to download", "body": "The ballot asks whether others can build on your work. Saying you made a roadmap is not enough. Offer the files: your interview question lists, the KAP survey forms, the stamp-card booth plan, and the LINE platform setup.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-ug-queens-02", "edu-2023-og-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "Each lists exactly what another team gets and how to reuse it.", "ballot": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?"}, {"id": "hp-070", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Lastly, ReLEAF demonstrates how synthetic biology can move beyond abstract lab research", "title": "Add limits before the big finish", "body": "Both 2025 winners have a section on limitations or negative impacts. Yours could list membrane leakage, the open kill switch decision, no field trial yet, GMO approval, and natural farmers' worry about over-intervention. Put it before this paragraph.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-05", "ent-2025-og-wur-05"], "excerpt_note": "NYU separates designed from proven; WageningenUR names a harm and a mitigation.", "ballot": "How thoughtfully was it implemented? How well did they explain the context, rationale and prior work?"}], "excerpts": {"edu-2023-og-epfl-02": {"id": "edu-2023-og-epfl-02", "prize": "education", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/epfl/education", "section": "BioQuest", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "The Git repository contains the .godot file, all the files required to build the game, and a README guide that explains how to build and run the game using GODOT.\nThe README also offers insights into game design, code, and instructions on how to utilize the template effectively. This resource not only enables gameplay but also facilitates easy modifications for future IGEM teams. You can also access the PDF of the readme below for your convenience.", "why_it_works": "Says exactly what is in the repository (.godot file, build files, README) and what the README covers, so a future team knows what it will get before opening the link.", "teaches": ["reusability"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Workshop Booth in Heidelberg's Old Town", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To measure our impact, we asked participants to mark their interest and self-assessed knowledge on a graph before and after the game. After analyzing the results, we found that interest increased by 20% and self-assessed knowledge grew by 56.3%. This shows that, with the right approach, the general public is interested in and willing to engage with science.", "why_it_works": "States the method in one sentence (mark interest and knowledge before and after) and the result in the next. Caveats for students copying it: the page gives no number of participants, and the figure caption says an unpaired test was used on before/after data.", "teaches": ["evidence", "method"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-ug-queens-02": {"id": "edu-2025-ug-queens-02", "prize": "education", "team": "Queens-Canada", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/queens-canada/education", "section": "Workshop 1:", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "All lesson plans, slide decks, and activity templates have been documented and donated to Let's Talk Science and our wiki so future teams can reuse or adapt them. Teams can run the same workshop, translate materials to other languages, or extend the activity into multi-day modules. Donated kits ensure quick setup and sustainable outreach use across classrooms.", "why_it_works": "Lists the exact materials handed over (lesson plans, slide decks, templates), who holds them, and three concrete ways another team can reuse them. This is what the ballot means by 'documented so others can build on it'.", "teaches": ["reusability", "structure"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-ug-queens-04": {"id": "edu-2025-ug-queens-04", "prize": "education", "team": "Queens-Canada", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/queens-canada/education", "section": "WiSE Workshop", "function": "reflection", "text": "While we initially avoided highly technical content out of concern it might alienate younger participants, our experiences with other outreach initiatives such as MiniQ and the Science Fair showed that children are highly receptive to learning seemingly complex ideas when presented in interactive, age-appropriate ways. Future workshops may integrate synthetic biology concepts through simplified models—for example, simulating genetic circuits with colored blocks or using storytelling to introduce biological “design challenges.”", "why_it_works": "Names an assumption the team held, the evidence from other events that overturned it, and the concrete change it leads to (colored blocks for genetic circuits). That is a full what-we-learned, what-changes loop in three sentences.", "teaches": ["reflection", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-ug-queens-06": {"id": "edu-2025-ug-queens-06", "prize": "education", "team": "Queens-Canada", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/queens-canada/education", "section": "Lab Workshop", "function": "method", "text": "The Discovery Lab Workshop provides high school students and community members with hands-on exposure to fundamental laboratory techniques through a structured 2.5-hour experience. Participants rotate through four stations: precision pipetting with data analysis, pH testing using litmus paper, anatomical dissection (eye specimens), and microscopy observation including gel electrophoresis results. Each 20-25 minute station combines demonstration with guided practice, ensuring students gain practical experience with essential lab skills while working in small groups with dedicated instructors.", "why_it_works": "A description a teacher could plan from: length (2.5 hours), number of stations (four), what each station is, and how long each takes (20 to 25 minutes). Numbers replace adjectives.", "teaches": ["clarity", "concision", "reusability"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-ug-queens-07": {"id": "edu-2025-ug-queens-07", "prize": "education", "team": "Queens-Canada", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/queens-canada/education", "section": "Genes to Genius", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "The video sparked curiosity and genuine dialogue generating close to 6,000 social media impressions. By progressing through increasing levels of complexity, we created space for audiences to ask questions and challenge their own assumptions. This structure promoted mutual learning and showed how synthetic biology can be accessible at all levels.", "why_it_works": "Counter-example: uses reach (6,000 impressions) as proof of 'genuine dialogue' and 'mutual learning'. An impression is one view on a feed. Report the comments or questions received, or call it reach.", "teaches": ["evidence", "claim-support"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "ent-2025-og-wur-05": {"id": "ent-2025-og-wur-05", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Exclusion of Small-scale Farmers", "function": "limitation", "text": "BCoated: Slow-release is set successfully enter the seed coating market. However, coating the seeds costs money, and thus our seeds will be sold at a premium compared to naked seeds. Due to this premium, our product may become inaccessible for small-scale farmers who have smaller budgets than large-scale farmers. As a result, small-scale farmers will have lower yields, making it even more difficult for them to compete with large-scale farmers. To prevent these social issues from occurring, we will stay in touch with these small-scale farmers and may make special price arrangements with seed coating companies that resell to small scale farmers.", "why_it_works": "A negative impact written as a chain: premium price, lower yields for small farms, wider gap. Then one concrete mitigation, not a reassurance.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "limitation"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ihp-2023-og-sdu-03": {"id": "ihp-2023-og-sdu-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "SDU-Denmark", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/sdu-denmark/human-practices", "section": "Avoid greenwashing", "function": "limitation", "text": "Since the vitroZymes currently have only been tested in their ability to degrade the molecule PFOA, we cannot allow water work companies to promote our product as a complete solution to removing PFAS from the environment as of right now. We need to underscore that vitroZymes only have the potential to degrade other molecules, but further investigation is required.", "why_it_works": "Limits the product claim to what was tested (PFOA only) and says what partners may not say about it. A rare passage that guards the page's claims against overreach.", "teaches": ["consistency", "limitation", "evidence"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2023-ug-hust-02": {"id": "ihp-2023-ug-hust-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "HUST-China", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/hust-china/human-practices", "section": "Section 6: On-site Research and Public Evaluation", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Surprisingly, we discovered that the flue gas emitted by the power plant, after undergoing desulfurization treatment, had an SO2 content of less than 30 mg/m³, and nearly all nitrogen compounds were removed. This meant that our initially designed resistant component might not be effective in practical production applications. After careful consideration, we decided to allocate more of our efforts to testing and enhancing lactate production and electricity generation.Nonetheless,our resistant component design could inspire future iGEM teams.", "why_it_works": "Field data (SO2 under 30 mg/m³) shows a planned module is not needed, and the team says so and moves the effort. Dropping work because of evidence is reported as a result.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "evidence", "consistency"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2023-ug-hust-03": {"id": "ihp-2023-ug-hust-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "HUST-China", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/hust-china/human-practices", "section": "5.1 Initial Hardware Design For Coal-Fired Power Plants", "function": "definition", "text": "During our discussions with Professor Li, he explained that the heat produced by industrial facilities can be categorized into high-quality heat that can be efficiently utilized and low-quality heat that cannot. Many existing applications predominantly make use of high-quality heat while neglecting the potential utility of low-quality heat, whereas our specific need for a cultivation temperature of 30℃ aligns with the potential utilization of low-quality heat. Professor Li suggested that we explore the use of low-quality waste heat from coal-fired power plants, such as waste heat from cooling towers, to maintain the required temperature for our microbial cultivation. This suggestion provided valuable inspiration for our hardware design.", "why_it_works": "Explains a specialist idea (high- vs low-quality heat) in plain words before using it, then shows why it fits the 30 ℃ culture need. The reader is never asked to know thermal engineering.", "teaches": ["assumed-knowledge", "clarity", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-01": {"id": "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-01", "prize": "ihp", "team": "Bielefeld-CeBiTec", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/bielefeld-cebitec/human-practices", "section": "Dr. Marco Radukic", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Incorporating the insights Dr. Radukic and advice from recent consultations, we adjusted the lipid-to-nucleic acid ratio from 22:1 to 10:1 to enhance efficiency and modified the pH and concentration of sodium acetate solutions for better packaging. Storage conditions were strictly managed at 4 °C, and pipetting/mixing techniques were refined to ensure quality consistency. Quality control was expanded to include fluorescence testing, zeta potential measurements, and light scattering, alongside cytotoxicity tests. These improvements not only address transfection challenges but also strengthen our foundation for future LNP applications.", "why_it_works": "Every change is stated as before and after with units: ratio 22:1 to 10:1, storage at 4 °C, added QC methods named one by one. A reader could repeat the protocol from this paragraph.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "concision"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-03": {"id": "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "Bielefeld-CeBiTec", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/bielefeld-cebitec/human-practices", "section": "Joshua Bauder", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "Joshua showed us just how dire the situation is for CF patients is in some regions. It was shocking to hear there is only one doctor knowledgeable about CF in Thailand and that many doctors dismiss the possibility of CF due to racial bias and misinformation. Additionally, we confirmed how much the accessibility of care depends on the healthcare system, as we already touched on during the interview with Nicole Friedlein . On the parenting level, Joshua brought in many perspectives contrary to what we previously heard. In the interview with Max , we learned he vehemently avoids ponding water while Joshua’s daughter is allowed to roam around with no such restrictions. Neither have chronic infections.", "why_it_works": "Sets two stakeholders' accounts side by side (Max avoids ponding water; Joshua's daughter roams without that restriction) and states that neither has chronic infections. Conflicting input is reported openly and cross-referenced to the earlier interview.", "teaches": ["consistency", "evidence", "structure"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-06": {"id": "ihp-2024-og-bielefeld-06", "prize": "ihp", "team": "Bielefeld-CeBiTec", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/bielefeld-cebitec/human-practices", "section": "Global Impact and Inclusivity", "function": "conclusion", "text": "Recognizing the disparities in CF care across different regions, particularly in underrepresented areas like Asia, we adjusted our approach to create a more inclusive therapy. With feedback from stakeholders like Joshua Bauder from CF Vest International and Dr. Sriram Vaidyanathan, we ensured our therapy addressed a wider range of CF mutations. This global focus led to bilingual surveys and expanded outreach efforts to raise awareness about CF and gene therapy.", "why_it_works": "Counter-example: the conclusion says the therapy 'addressed a wider range of CF mutations', but the Joshua Bauder entry says a change of target was 'not being feasible anymore' and F508del stayed. A conclusion must not claim more than the entries it summarises.", "teaches": ["consistency", "evidence"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-og-uzh-02": {"id": "ihp-2024-og-uzh-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "UZurich", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uzurich/integrated-human-practices", "section": "Claudia Vaderna - Schweizer Allianz Gentechfrei (Swiss Alliance for a GMO-free agriculture)", "function": "reflection", "text": "The exchange with Claudia Vaderna showed us that we have a responsibility to study the biosafety and the ecological impact of our project. For GMO and non-GMO crops to coexist, our bacterial strain would have to be maintained in the field without the risk of entering neighboring (agro-)ecosystems. It also showed us that a potential product would have to be tested for its impact on soil biodiversity before being applied in the field. We were vindicated in our decision to incorporate a sensing mechanism and kill switch into our bacteria to ensure biosafety, but we learned that we would need to conduct a lot of safety testing before we could responsibly apply a product like RhyzUp.", "why_it_works": "Supplementary case. A critic of GMOs is quoted fairly and her concerns become concrete testing obligations. The team states what was confirmed and what it still owes before release.", "teaches": ["reflection", "consistency", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-01": {"id": "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-01", "prize": "ihp", "team": "JU-Krakow", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ju-krakow/human-practices", "section": "Our human practices in a nutshell", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "Who we contacted:\nDr Paweł Brzezowski\nWhy:\nWe encountered some problems with stabilising the culture of Prymnesium parvum.\nWhat we learned:\n- We realized the importance of maintaining the pH of the F/2 medium.\nWhat we adapted to our project:\nThanks to his recommendations, we adjusted the pH of the medium from 5.5 to 7.3.", "why_it_works": "The whole loop in 55 words under four fixed labels, ending in a number a reader can check (pH 5.5 to 7.3). A model for short summary cards.", "teaches": ["concision", "structure", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-02": {"id": "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "JU-Krakow", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ju-krakow/human-practices", "section": "18-21 April 2024 – SEMPOWISKO – Interdisciplinary Math-Science Students Conference", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "After a joint discussion and reevaluating the rapid notification app's usefulness, we concluded that the existing government notification system seems sufficient. It is managed by experts, and alerts about threats are issued when algal quantity thresholds are exceeded, or when fish die-offs occur. Government organisations then send text messages and regularly publish reports on their official website [7]. Our app aimed to facilitate collaboration between state services, scientists, and the fishing community. However, we realised that making it available for public use could lead to social panic. Allowing inexperienced individuals to input data from our test could result in tracking false positive results. Considering the safety and practical aspects, we decided to abandon this idea.", "why_it_works": "An audience question leads to a feature being dropped. The paragraph gives the existing alternative, the risk (false positives, panic) and the decision, so the cut reads as judgement.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "reflection", "clarity"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-04": {"id": "ihp-2024-ug-jukrakow-04", "prize": "ihp", "team": "JU-Krakow", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ju-krakow/human-practices", "section": "Social acceptance of Prymnesium parvum testing", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "During the Symbioza conference, one of last year's iGEM members from the DTU-Denmark team – Konrad Uściło, raised our awareness of the potential risk of causing public panic by introducing a Lateral Flow test for self-testing of environmental samples. This valuable insight and significant concern prompted us to consult our supervisors. Dr Paweł Jedynak, who has extensive experience in science outreach and science communication, advised us to compare our test to something the general public is familiar with (e.g., a thermometer) when speaking with them. For this reason, we chose to liken our test to a pregnancy test, as it is a well-known item with a similar function and appearance. We also decided to be very cautious when considering the potential distribution of our test in the future. It would require a well-organised educational campaign to minimise the risk of causing panic.", "why_it_works": "A social risk (public panic) gets the same treatment as a lab problem: who raised it, who they asked next, and two concrete responses. The analogy is chosen for a named audience.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "assumed-knowledge", "audience-framing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-02": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#2 Key considerations for the design of the coatings", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Dr. Desalegn W. Etalo from WUR Translational Phytopathology warned us that microbial inoculants can disrupt soil communities, either being rejected by the local community or outcompeting native microbes. Instead, he suggested using precursor molecules to “fish” for beneficial microbes.\nThis led us to Prof. Dr. Jonne Rodenburg from the chair group Weed Ecology at WUR. He confirmed the potential of incorporating a precursor that mitigates against disastrous parasitic weeds, preventing crop damage. This became our first proof-of-concept use case: demonstrating parasite control through modular BC coatings. This can provide a simple solution for farmers worldwide. Currently, these farmers have to rely on labour-intensive methods that can do nothing more than mitigate losses.", "why_it_works": "A chain of named people: one expert warns and suggests, the suggestion leads to a second expert, and his confirmation becomes the first use case. Each sentence hands the reader to the next step with 'Instead', 'This led us to', 'This became'.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "structure", "clarity"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-03": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#3 Choice of the platform material for the coating and EU regulations", "function": "definition", "text": "Traditionally, seed coatings have relied on petroleum-based binders (adhesives, or “seed stickers”). This is a practice soon to be outlawed, as we found out from our stakeholders in the seed sector. Our consultation with Amalia Kafka from Euroseeds highlighted the urgency of this change, due to the upcoming EU microplastics restrictions under the EU chemical legislation REACH:\n- By 2028: deliberately added microplastics will be restricted in agricultural products, including seed treatments.\n- By 2031: the restriction will extend to all plant protection products and treated seeds.", "why_it_works": "Glosses the trade term in the same sentence ('binders (adhesives, or “seed stickers”)'), names the source, and turns the regulation into two dated bullets a reader can check.", "teaches": ["assumed-knowledge", "evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-04": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-04", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#7.1 Business plan and scaling up", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "To explore industrial feasibility, we visited Bio Base Europe Pilot Plant in Ghent, gaining first-hand insight into scale-up processes and how early design choices impact large-scale production. We discussed the costs of piloting and learned how this is the developmental phase where many start-ups fail. To decrease the costs of piloting, we could use a pilot plant facility, such as Bio Base Europe. We would have to use a production design for which an infrastructure exists. Therefore, we decided to change our production plan from the original design—consisting of stacked trays as suggested by Dr. Amritpal Singh—to a stirred tank reactor. This might slightly decrease the yields, but the capital expenditures are not worth the increase in yield.", "why_it_works": "Reports a design change to the production plan (stacked trays to stirred tank) with the reason and the cost of the change. Admitting the yield loss makes the decision credible.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "evidence", "reflection"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-h01": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-h01", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "Amalia Kafka", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "Why we reached out: As a seed lobbying group, they represent the interests of active stakeholders in the research, breeding, production, and marketing of seeds and seed technologies for various plant species.\n\nMain takeaways: Availability of biodegradable active compounds within seed coats is a problem. However, the development of seed coating materials without microplastics has been in progress for a while. More time is needed for these novel products to be implemented.\n\nReflection & integration: There have already been efforts from the market to transition into biodegradable coatings; however, the deadline for the regulations is approaching, and time is limited. This made us realise that collaboration with businesses that have experience, equipment and infrastructure will be vital in meeting the deadline. Additionally, we realised that only having a biodegradable material is not novel, and our value proposition should highlight the ease of incorporating desired active compounds into the coating.", "why_it_works": "The full three-slot template in 149 words. The reflection turns one market fact into two decisions: find partners who already have equipment, and pitch how easily active compounds go into the coating, since a biodegradable material alone is not new.", "teaches": ["structure", "loop-closing", "concision"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-h04": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-h04", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "Anonymous arable farmer", "function": "reflection", "text": "Reflection & integration: The unpredictability in weather is frustrating to farmers, and we should come up with a solution that would, for example, prevent rotting when flooding occurs—such as tuning the water-holding capacity or hydrophobicity of the coatings.\n\nThere is a gap between the regulatory requirements and the struggles of farmers. We should consider this when complying with new policies, avoid neglecting the impacts on farmers, and develop mitigation strategies in collaboration with other sector stakeholders.", "why_it_works": "Turns a farmer's complaint about flooding into two coating properties the team can tune, and names a gap between regulators and farmers. The second paragraph is a commitment, and the page would be stronger if it said what was done about it.", "teaches": ["audience-framing", "reflection", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-02": {"id": "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "NYU-Abu-Dhabi", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/nyu-abu-dhabi/human-practices", "section": "What We Learned", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "We consulted protein engineering researcher Dr. Jin Montclare for guidance on our PMI inhibition strategy. She raised substantial safety concerns we had underestimated: PMI is involved in multiple biological pathways beyond mannose metabolism, creating potential for harmful cascading effects across interconnected metabolic networks. Blocking this enzyme risked unintended consequences we couldn't predict or control. She questioned whether direct Man-1-P supplementation might bypass these risks entirely while achieving our goal more safely. This reframed our thinking—instead of manipulating a fundamental metabolic enzyme with uncertain downstream effects in an already vulnerable patient population, we could supplement the desired product directly.", "why_it_works": "One paragraph runs the full loop: who was asked, the specific risk she raised (PMI sits in several pathways), the alternative she proposed, and the new direction. It sits between 'Initial Approach' and 'The Pivot', so the reader always knows which step they are in.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "structure", "clarity"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03": {"id": "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "NYU-Abu-Dhabi", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/nyu-abu-dhabi/human-practices", "section": "Campus CDG Awareness Survey", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To understand awareness levels in our own community, we conducted a campus survey of 97 participants. Only 9 (9.2%) had heard of CDG before, quantifying the education gap we needed to address. However, over 80% expressed interest in learning more about rare diseases. When asked about preferred learning formats, 25% requested infographics and posters, approximately 35% preferred short video content, and over 40% requested on-campus events and workshops—insights that directly shaped our educational outreach approach.", "why_it_works": "Gives the sample size, the raw count and the percentage, then ties each format preference to the outreach that followed. Every number is there to justify a decision.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "concision"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-05": {"id": "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-05", "prize": "ihp", "team": "NYU-Abu-Dhabi", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/nyu-abu-dhabi/human-practices", "section": "Limitations, Future Directions, and Conclusion", "function": "limitation", "text": "Experimental Validation: Our partnership with Dr. Kozicz at Mount Sinai established a validation pathway using patient-derived fibroblasts, but timeline constraints prevented completing these crucial experiments. The enzyme activity assays and glycosylation measurements that would demonstrate our bEV-delivered PMM2 approach's efficacy remain future work. We designed constructs and optimized delivery mechanisms, but proof-of-concept in authentic disease models awaits execution.", "why_it_works": "Says plainly which experiments were not done and what result is still missing. It separates what was designed from what was proven, which keeps the page's claims consistent with its results.", "teaches": ["consistency", "evidence", "limitation"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-h03": {"id": "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-h03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "NYU-Abu-Dhabi", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/nyu-abu-dhabi/human-practices", "section": "Industry Insights from the Frontlines: Consultation with Dr. Hicham Alaoui of Glycomine", "function": "reflection", "text": "This consultation provided a crucial industry perspective that both validated and challenged our approach. Dr. Alaoui's explanation of why Glycomine chose Man-1-P over GDP-mannose—prioritizing molecular simplicity—countered some of Dr. Andreotti's skepticism about supplementation efficacy. However, his confirmation of Man-1-P's blood instability and rapid enzymatic degradation highlighted a fundamental challenge: even if we successfully biomanufactured Man-1-P, delivering it effectively to cells remained problematic. His mention of chemical production costs and suggestion to explore in vitro enzymatic synthesis validated our biomanufacturing direction as potentially valuable for reducing therapeutic costs.", "why_it_works": "Weighs one expert against another by name: Alaoui's reason for choosing Man-1-P answers part of Andreotti's doubt, and his half-life point raises a new one. Each sentence pairs what he said with what it meant for the plan.", "teaches": ["reflection", "loop-closing", "evidence"], "prizeName": "IHP"}}};
